          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p1\",\"quote\":\"The first one corresponds to the sidereal rotation period (or sidereal day ), i.e., the time that the object takes to complete a full rotation around its axis relative to the background stars ( inertial space ). The other type of commonly used \\\"rotation period\\\" is the object's synodic rotation period (or solar day ), which may differ, by a fraction of a rotation or more than one rotation, to accommodate the portion of the object's orbital period around a star or another body during one day.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p2\",\"quote\":\"Typically, the stated rotation period for a giant planet (such as Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune) is its internal rotation period, as determined from the rotation of the planet's magnetic field .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p37\",\"quote\":\"Uranus −0.71833 days [ 3 ] [ ii ] −0 d 17 h 14 m 24 s −0.718 32 d (−17 h 14 m 23 s) [ 10 ]\"}]}"
